
1. Introduction to Linux
Linux is an open-source operating system widely used
in servers, cloud computing, and software development.
It provides a command-line interface to interact with
the system using different commands.

2. File and Directory Commands
The pwd command displays the present working directory.
The ls command lists files and directories.
The cd command is used to change directories.
The mkdir command creates a new directory.
The touch command creates an empty file.
The cp command copies files and directories.
The mv command moves or renames files.
The rm command removes files and directories.

3. File Viewing Commands
The cat command displays the complete contents of a file.
The head command displays the first ten lines by default.
The tail command displays the last ten lines by default.
The more command displays file content one screen at a time.
The less command allows forward and backward navigation.
These commands are useful when working with large files.

4. User Management
The useradd command creates a new user.
The passwd command changes a user's password.
The usermod command modifies an existing user account.
The userdel command deletes a user account.
The id command displays user and group information.
The whoami command displays the current username.

5. Group Management
The groupadd command creates a new group.
The groupdel command deletes a group.
The groups command displays group memberships.
The getent group command displays information about groups.
Users can belong to multiple supplementary groups.
A user's primary group is assigned to their account.

6. File Permissions
Linux permissions control access to files and directories.
The chmod command changes file permissions.
The chown command changes file ownership.
The chgrp command changes group ownership.
Permissions include read, write, and execute.
The symbols r, w, and x represent these permissions.

7. Searching Files
The find command searches for files and directories.
The grep command searches for text inside files.
The grep -r command searches directories recursively.
The grep -l command displays filenames with matches.
The locate command can find files using an indexed database.

8. Process Management
The ps command displays information about processes.
The top command displays running processes dynamically.
The kill command sends a signal to a process.
The jobs command displays jobs in the current shell.
The bg command resumes a job in the background.
The fg command brings a job to the foreground.

9. Disk and Memory Information
The df command displays filesystem disk space.
The du command estimates file and directory disk usage.
The free command displays RAM and swap usage.
The free -h command displays memory in readable units.
The uptime command shows how long the system has been running.

10. Networking Commands
The ping command tests network connectivity.
The ip addr command displays network interface addresses.
The hostname command displays the system hostname.
The ss command displays socket information.
The curl command transfers data using URLs.

11. Text Processing
The awk command processes text using fields and patterns.
The sed command performs text transformations.
The cut command extracts selected fields.
The sort command sorts lines of text.
The uniq command identifies adjacent duplicate lines.
The wc command counts lines, words, and bytes.
The tr command translates or deletes characters.

12. Redirection and Pipes
The greater-than symbol redirects output to a file.
The double greater-than symbol appends output.
The pipe symbol sends one command's output to another.
The less-than symbol redirects input from a file.
The tee command writes output to a file and the terminal.

13. Package Management
Package managers install and remove software.
Ubuntu uses tools such as apt.
RHEL-based systems commonly use dnf or yum.
Always check the package name before installing software.
Administrative privileges may be required for installation.

14. System Administration
The date command displays the current date and time.
The cal command displays a calendar.
The uname command displays system information.
The hostnamectl command displays hostname details.
The systemctl command manages system services.
The journalctl command displays system journal logs.

15. Conclusion
Linux commands help administrators manage users, files,
permissions, processes, memory, and network connections.
Practicing these commands regularly improves confidence.
Use the manual pages with the man command to learn
about available options and examples for each command.

